ilter(condition=[AND(=($1(inv_item_sk), $4(i_item_sk)), =($2(inv_warehouse_sk), $26(w_warehouse_sk)), =($0(inv_date_sk), $40(d_date_sk)), =($46(d_year), 1999), OR(=(CAST($16(i_category)):CHAR(5), 'Books'), =(CAST($16(i_category)):CHAR(5), 'Shoes')), >=($24(i_manager_id), 81), <=($24(i_manager_id), 100), >=($3(inv_quantity_on_hand), 800), <=($3(inv_quantity_on_hand), 1000))])\n                    LogicalJoin(condition=[true], joinType=[inner])\n                      LogicalJoin(condition=[true], joinType=[inner])\n                        LogicalJoin(condition=[true], joinType=[inner])\n                          LogicalTableScan(table=[[inventory]])\n                          LogicalTableScan(table=[[item]])\n                        LogicalTableScan(table=[[warehouse]])\n                      LogicalTableScan(table=[[date_dim]])\n  \n```"}], 'response': "Step 1: The query rewrite rule applies to the given SQL query by identifying the relational expression changes made by the rule.\n\nStep 2: Parsing the logical plan changes into detailed changes of the given SQL query, we can see that the query contains a `GROUP BY` clause and aggregate functions operating directly on columns. The `SELECT` clause only contains the columns used in the `GROUP BY` clause and the aggregates without any transformations or additional expressions.\n\nStep 3: Since the query meets the conditions of Case 1 in the query rewrite rule, we can apply the transformations specified in that case.\n\nStep 4: According to the query rewrite rule, Case 1 applies when a SQL query contains a `GROUP BY` clause that groups by simple columns (no expressions in the `GROUP BY` list), and the aggregate functions operate directly on columns. The `SELECT` clause only contains the columns used in the `GROUP BY` clause and the aggregates without any transformations or additional expressions.\n\nThe detailed changes of the given SQL query are as follows:\n\n- Directly apply the `GROUP BY` and aggregate functions to the data source referred to in the `FROM` clause of the inner query before the projection.\n- Eliminate the outer projection layer if it only renames the columns without transforming them.\n\nBy applying these transformations, we can rewrite the given SQL query as follows:\n\n```sql\nSELECT \n  w_warehouse_name,\n  w_warehouse_sk,\n  i_item_sk,\n  d_moy,\n  stdev,\n  mean,\n  cov\nFROM (\n  SELECT \n    w_warehouse_name,\n    w_warehouse_sk,\n    i_item_sk,\n    d_moy,\n    stddev_samp(inv_quantity_on_hand) AS stdev,\n    avg(inv_quantity_on_hand) AS mean,\n    case when mean = 0 then null else stddev/mean end as cov\n  FROM \n    inventory, item, warehouse, date_dim\n  WHERE \n    inv_item_sk = i_item_sk\n    and inv_warehouse_sk = w_warehouse_sk\n    and inv_date_sk = d_date_sk\n    and d_year = 1999\n    and i_category IN ('Books', 'Shoes')\n    and i_manager_id BETWEEN 81 AND 100\n    and inv_quantity_on_hand BETWEEN 800 AND 1000\n  GROUP BY \n    w_warehouse_name, w_warehouse_sk, i_item_sk, d_moy\n) AS foo\nWHERE \n  cov > 1.5;\n```\n\nThe rewritten query applies the `GROUP BY` and aggregate functions directly to the data source referred to in the `FROM` clause of the inner query before the projection, eliminating the outer projection layer if it only renames the columns without transforming them.", 'time': 12.850621938705444}
