n does not provide any specific rules for rewriting the SQL query, so we will focus on the existing rewrite rules.\n\nStep 2: Based on our evaluation of the rewrite rules, we can apply the following transformations:\n\n1. Apply Rule FILTER_INTO_JOIN to move filter conditions that do not reference columns from both tables involved in the join into the ON clause of the INNER JOIN or as a WHERE clause on a subquery of the respective side.\n2. Apply Rule PROJECT_REDUCE_EXPRESSIONS to simplify expressions and eliminate redundancy.\n3. Apply Rule AGGREGATE_REDUCE_FUNCTIONS to reduce aggregate functions and eliminate unnecessary calculations.\n4. Apply Rule PROJECT_FILTER_TRANSPOSE to move conditions from the WHERE clause to a subquery that the SELECT clause is applied on.\n\nThe rewritten SQL query would be:\n\n```sql\nSELECT \n    col1, \n    transformation_function(col2) \nFROM (\n    SELECT \n        col1, \n        col2 \n    FROM table \n    WHERE condition\n) AS subquery;\n```\n\nNote: The above transformations are based on the assumption that the given SQL query is a simplified representation of the actual query and may not be applicable to all scenarios.', 'time': 5.406813144683838}
